## Context

Ticket 0026 was ruled a monster on 2026-09-02 and decomposed by ladder rung;
this is rung 1 of five children, and 0026 is the tracker. Goal 1 is the
cheapest rung on the ladder (README.md, "## The goals ladder"): its assertions
need no corpus, no build and no library, so they are decidable the moment the
system is installed. The ruled method is tests first, bottom-up — a rung is
declared only when its assertions run, which is why the evidence column on
README.md is the test column.

Three of goal 1's four terms are this ticket's, plus one clause that belongs to
a higher rung and is built here anyway. R10 and R15 are goal 1's; R22 stays on
goal 1's roster but is owned by ticket 0033, and R31 by ticket 0488 — neither is
this ticket's work, and the ladder's roster is not a claim about who builds it.
The fourth item is R19's normalizer sweep, which sits on goal 3 and is built
with this batch because it is the cheapest gate on the sheet (0026's log,
2026-08-31T20:08Z) and needs nothing a goal-1 assertion does not already need.
R19's other half — the two normalizers agreeing at index time — needs a built
index and stays with ticket 0580.

`bench/smoke_upstream.py` already carries two of these against stock upstream at
the reviewed SHA in `UPSTREAM`: `check_local_by_default` (R10, the default
embedder is local, asserted against a running server rather than the source) and
`check_model_stays_in_data_dir` (R15's model-cache clause, inherited when R28
merged into R15). What is missing is R10's second half, R15's deletion clause,
and the sweep.

## Relevant files

- `bench/smoke_upstream.py` — the gate this ticket extends; its `check()`
  helper is the assertion record (id, requirement, clause, falsified_by).
- `bench/fold_sweep.mjs` — the R19 sweep. Today it always exits 0, prints a
  WARNING, and crashes on any tree lacking `normalizeForSearch`.
- `bench/results/smoke-1.12.0/checks.json` — where the run's verdicts land.
- `UPSTREAM` — the reviewed SHA and the repository URLs; `make upstream-checkout`
  recreates the git-ignored `fork/` at it.
- `SPEC.md` §5.2.8 — the fold gate's specification. Cite it; do not restate it.
- `README.md`, "## Goal 1 — I can install it and take it off again" — the rung's
  roster and the level each term is decided at.

## Actions

1. R10's second clause: my library text and my queries stay on this machine
   without an opt-in. The existing check establishes that the default embedder
   is local; the clause is about egress, so assert it as egress — no network
   destination outside the machine is contacted on a default-configured run
   that indexes and answers.
2. R15's deletion clause: deleting the data directory is the whole uninstall.
   Event-then-state, per 0026's log of 2026-08-31T20:08Z — observe the deletion
   as an event and then assert the state that follows it, rather than asserting a
   state that a prior run could also have produced. The residue to look for is
   anything the install wrote outside the data directory.
3. The R19 codepoint sweep: give `fold_sweep.mjs` the classification fallback
   (`tokenize`-only when `normalizeForSearch` is absent, so a pre-fold tree is
   red by a recorded miss count and not by a crash), nonzero exit codes, and a
   writable built checkout to point at. The scripts it must cover are R7's, read
   from the sheet rather than restated here; 0026's log of 2026-08-31T13:38Z
   records why Arabic, Devanagari and Chinese break a Latin-plus-Cyrillic sweep's
   assumptions.
4. Wire the batch into the Makefile with honest states. A gate that cannot look
   reports that it could not look.

## Test

Red first, and the red state has to be produced rather than argued. For the
sweep that is a pre-fold tree: check out upstream below v1.7.2, run the gate,
and see a nonzero exit with a miss count — the fallback path is exactly the one
no green run exercises. For R15's deletion clause the red fixture is an install
that writes a file outside the data directory, planted on purpose. For R10 the
red fixture is a configuration that opts egress in: the assertion must go red
there, or it is asserting the default rather than the clause.

## Verification

- [ ] Each new assertion appears in `bench/results/smoke-1.12.0/checks.json`
      with its own id, requirement and `falsified_by` string.
- [ ] Each was seen red once, against the fixture named in Test, before it was
      seen green.
- [ ] `fold_sweep.mjs` exits nonzero on a pre-fold tree and zero against stock
      at the reviewed SHA, and neither outcome is a crash.
- [ ] `make check` green; `./tickets/erg check` green.

## Invariants

- The unit is the MUST clause, not the requirement: one assertion per clause, so
  README.md's `delivered` column becomes a count rather than a reader's
  judgement. Inherited from the tracker, ticket 0026.
- Honest red and waiver states in the Makefile, never a green that means "could
  not look". Inherited from the tracker, ticket 0026.
- No threshold, budget or design number is restated here. SPEC.md §5.2.8 owns
  the gates and §5.2.9 the budgets; this ticket cites addresses.
- The gate runs against stock upstream at the reviewed SHA in `UPSTREAM` and
  changes no upstream code.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] R10's egress clause asserted, seen red against an opted-in fixture
- [ ] R15's deletion clause asserted event-then-state, seen red against a
      planted out-of-directory residue
- [ ] R19's codepoint sweep classifies instead of crashing, exits nonzero on a
      pre-fold tree, and covers the script classes R7 declares
- [ ] the three run from the Makefile with honest red/waiver states
- [ ] README.md's goal-1 rows carry an evidence verdict that rests on something
      that ran, for the terms this ticket asserts
